Reviewing responsive layout issues in the CSS.

Reviewing responsive layout issues in the CSS.

Done

Good — variables and box-sizing are in place. One important fix before we move on:

Use max-width, not width, for .container.
You have width: $container-width (1200px) — that's a fixed width that will overflow the viewport at your smaller required resolutions (1024×768, 768×1024). If the width is fixed, the browser will just add a horizontal scrollbar instead of shrinking the container. max-width says "grow up to this width but shrink if the screen is narrower" — combine it with width: 100% so it fills the available space up to that cap:

.container {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: $container-width;
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
}

This is the actual mechanism that will make your responsiveness requirement (#9) work correctly on the smaller resolutions later, so it's worth having right from the start.

A small style note (optional, not required): header, section, footer all have identical padding rules right now — repeated three times. You can group selectors with a comma to write it once:

header, section, footer {
    padding-top: $section-padding;
    padding-bottom: $section-padding;
}

Not a SCSS-specific feature (plain CSS supports this too), just cleaner. Feel free to keep them separate if you'd rather add per-element overrides later — either works.

Fix the .container width and we'll move to Step 3: the navbar.

Step 3: Navbar markup + fixed positioning

Concept: "sticky to the top of the window when scrolling" means position: fixed (not the CSS position: sticky — fixed anchors it to the viewport, which is what you want for a navbar that overlays content as you scroll past it, matching the example gif). Fixed elements are taken out of normal document flow, so they'll overlap whatever's beneath them unless you account for it.

What to build:

Inside your <nav>, add a <ul> with one <li><a href="#...">...</a></li> per section (About, Host Cities, Highlights, Stadiums, Teams, Video) — the href must match the section ids exactly.

In SCSS, give nav:
position: fixed, top: 0, left: 0, width: 100%

A background color and z-index (needs to sit above your header/sections — try z-index: 1000 or similar)

Style the <ul> as a horizontal flex row (display: flex) instead of the default bulleted vertical list, and remove default list-style and margin/padding on the ul

Important gotcha: because nav is position: fixed, it no longer takes up space in the document — your <header> will now render underneath it, hidden behind the navbar. Add padding-top (or margin-top) to whatever comes right after nav (your <header>) roughly equal to your navbar's height, so content starts below the navbar instead of behind it.

Try building the nav markup and basic fixed styling, then post both files — we'll refine the flex layout and spacing, then move to smooth scrolling (Step 5) and the resize/indicator behavior (Steps 3-4 from the original list) after that.

