column2:
9/16/2026 12:10:36

Reviewing the SCSS for missing navigation markup.

Reviewing the SCSS for missing navigation markup.

Done

Good progress on the fixed positioning. A few things to fix/finish:

1. You didn't post the updated index.html — I asked for the nav <ul>/<li>/<a> markup too. Make sure you add that (links matching your section ids) and repost it alongside the SCSS.

2. Your ul isn't laid out horizontally yet. You set display: flex on nav, but nav will only have one direct child (the <ul>), so flexing nav doesn't do much — the <ul>'s own children (<li>s) are what need to lay out horizontally. Move the flex display to the ul itself:

ul {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex; // lays out <li> children in a row
}

You can keep justify-content off for now (default flex-start) or add spacing — try gap: 24px on the ul for even spacing between links, simpler than margins on each li.

3. The "content hidden behind fixed nav" gotcha from Step 3 isn't fixed yet. Your nav has no explicit height, so you don't know its pixel height to offset — that's fine, you don't need a magic number. Instead, give nav some padding (e.g. padding: 20px 0) so it has real height from its content, and give header a margin-top (or padding-top) that's a reasonable guess (e.g. 100px) to clear it. It doesn't have to be pixel-perfect right now — we'll revisit exact sizing once real nav content/font-size is in place, since Step 4 (navbar resizing) is going to change the nav's height dynamically anyway.

4. Style the <a> tags — right now your links will render with default blue/underline styling. Add a rule like:

nav a {
    color: $text-color;
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 600;
}

Post both the updated index.html (with nav links) and main.scss once you've made these changes, and we'll check the layout before moving to Step 5: smooth scrolling (a one-line CSS property, easiest win) and then tackle the harder JS-driven pieces — position indicator and navbar resizing — together, since they share the same scroll-event listener.

