column2:
9/16/2026 12:33:45

Everything here looks correct — nav is centered, header clears the fixed nav, smooth scroll is in. Let's move to the JS.

Step 6: Scroll-driven navbar (resize + position indicator)

These two features share one scroll event listener, so let's build it in layers rather than all at once.

Layer 1 — Get your element references

At the top of main.js, grab the DOM nodes you'll need repeatedly. Query them once outside the event handler (querying inside a scroll handler that fires dozens of times per second is wasteful):

const nav = document.querySelector('nav');
const sections = document.querySelectorAll('section');
const navLinks = document.querySelectorAll('nav a');

Note sections and navLinks are both NodeLists in the same order as they appear in your HTML — that's intentional, since we'll pair them up by index later (link i corresponds to section i).

Layer 2 — The scroll listener skeleton

window.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
    // logic goes here
});

Everything else lives inside this callback. Try adding just this shell with a console.log(window.scrollY) inside it first — confirm in your browser console that it fires and the number increases as you scroll. Getting this verified now saves confusion later if something doesn't work.

Layer 3 — Navbar resizing (the simpler of the two)

Concept: pick a scroll threshold (e.g. 50 pixels). Below it, nav is "large"; above it, nav is "small." Rather than manually setting styles in JS, toggle a CSS class and let SCSS own the actual sizes — that keeps your styling in one place:

if (window.scrollY > 50) {
    nav.classList.add('scrolled');
} else {
    nav.classList.remove('scrolled');
}

Then in main.scss, define what .scrolled changes — smaller padding and smaller font-size than the default:

nav {
    // ...existing rules...
    transition: padding 0.3s ease, font-size 0.3s ease;
    font-size: 20px;

    &.scrolled {
        padding: 8px 0;
        font-size: 14px;
    }
}

The transition property is what makes it animate smoothly instead of snapping — and &.scrolled is SCSS nesting syntax (compiles to nav.scrolled), a good second SCSS feature to point at in your writeup alongside variables.

Your task for this message: add Layers 1–3 (element refs, scroll listener, resize toggle + matching SCSS) and confirm the navbar visibly shrinks/grows as you scroll past 50px. Don't tackle the position indicator yet — post what you have for these three layers first so we can verify the resize works before stacking more logic into the same scroll handler.

