column2:
9/16/2026 12:42:44

This works correctly. One small housekeeping note: remove that console.log(window.scrollY) line once you've confirmed it's firing — leftover console logs are harmless but sloppy in a submission, and scroll handlers fire very frequently so logging on every one will flood your console while testing later features.

Step 7: Position indicator (active section highlighting)

This is the more involved piece. We'll build it in two parts: figuring out which section is "active", then reflecting that on the nav links.

Part A — Which section is under the navbar?

The core technique: each <section> (and your <header>) has a position on the page you can measure with getBoundingClientRect(). This returns the element's position relative to the viewport — so rect.top tells you how far the top of that section is from the top of the visible window, updated live as you scroll.

The logic: loop through your sections, and find the one whose top edge has scrolled up past (or is closest to) the bottom of your navbar. Something like:

function getCurrentSection() {
    const navHeight = nav.offsetHeight;
    let current = sections[0];

    sections.forEach((section) => {
        const rect = section.getBoundingClientRect();
        if (rect.top <= navHeight) {
            current = section;
        }
    });

    return current;
}

Walk through why this works: as you scroll down, more and more sections will have already scrolled past the navbar (rect.top <= navHeight becomes true for them in order, top to bottom). The loop doesn't break early — it keeps overwriting current — so by the end, current holds the last section satisfying the condition, i.e. the most recently-passed one. That's your active section.

Try this part first, in isolation — call console.log(getCurrentSection().id) inside your scroll listener and confirm the logged id changes correctly as you scroll through the page, matching the section actually near the top of your viewport. Get this verified before moving to Part B (which uses this section's id to highlight the matching nav link) — it's much easier to debug one piece at a time than to write both and guess which part broke if the highlighting doesn't work.

Post back once you've confirmed the console logs look right, and we'll wire up the actual link-highlighting next.

