column2:
9/16/2026 12:47:52

Great, that confirms Part A works. Now let's wire it to the nav links.

Part B — Highlighting the matching nav link

The pairing trick: your sections and navLinks NodeLists are in the same order (About is sections[0] and navLinks[0], Host Cities is sections[1] and navLinks[1], etc.) — since you built the HTML that way. So once you know the active section's index, you can use that same index into navLinks to know which link to highlight. You don't need to match by id/string at all if the ordering holds.

Update getCurrentSection to also give you the index, since that's what you actually need for the highlighting step. Simplest way: use a regular for loop instead of forEach so you have access to the index directly:

function getCurrentSectionIndex() {
    const navHeight = nav.offsetHeight;
    let currentIndex = 0;

    for (let i = 0; i < sections.length; i++) {
        const rect = sections[i].getBoundingClientRect();
        if (rect.top <= navHeight) {
            currentIndex = i;
        }
    }

    return currentIndex;
}

Same logic as before, just returning i instead of the section element.

Then, in your scroll listener, use that index to toggle an active class on the matching link — but first you need to clear the class from all links, otherwise old highlights never go away:

const activeIndex = getCurrentSectionIndex();

navLinks.forEach((link, i) => {
    if (i === activeIndex) {
        link.classList.add('active');
    } else {
        link.classList.remove('active');
    }
});

And the CSS — add an .active rule for nav a:

nav a {
    color: $text-color;
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 600;

    &.active {
        color: $primary-accent;
    }
}

One thing the assignment spec calls out specifically: make sure the last link highlights when you scroll to the very bottom of the page. Think about whether your current logic actually handles this — your loop only checks <section> elements, but your <footer> (below the last section) isn't in that list. If a user scrolls all the way down into the footer, does currentIndex still correctly point at your last section/link, or does it get "stuck" somewhere wrong? Test it by scrolling all the way to the bottom and checking the console/highlight — if it's already working correctly as-is, explain to yourself why it works (it's a good sign you understand the loop); if it's not, that's the next thing to fix.

Try this and report back what happens at the bottom of the page specifically.

9/16/2026 13:00:05

Good — that confirms the diagnosis. window.scrollY maxes out at 710, which now exactly equals documentElement.scrollHeight - innerHeight. So the page genuinely cannot scroll any further than that point — there's no bug in your getCurrentSection loop itself, there's just not enough page height below "stadiums" for teams's and video's tops to ever cross the navbar threshold, because those sections are still empty placeholder <div>s with no real content giving them height.

So: this will very likely resolve itself once you add real content to teams and video (cards, video embed, etc. — each with padding/height of its own), since that will push the total page height up and give the browser room to scroll those sections' tops past the navbar.

That said, it's still worth handling the edge case explicitly, per the assignment's specific callout: "make sure your implementation highlights the last menu item when you scroll to the bottom of the page." Even with full content, there's often a few leftover pixels at the very bottom (inside your <footer>, past the last <section>) where the loop's condition might not trigger for the last section if the last section is short. A common defensive fix: explicitly check if the user has scrolled to the bottom of the page (not just past a section threshold), and force the last link active in that case:

function getCurrentSectionIndex() {
    const navHeight = nav.offsetHeight;

    // Special case: scrolled to the very bottom of the page
    const atBottom = window.innerHeight + window.scrollY >= document.documentElement.scrollHeight - 1;
    if (atBottom) {
        return sections.length - 1;
    }

    let currentIndex = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < sections.length; i++) {
        const rect = sections[i].getBoundingClientRect();
        if (rect.top <= navHeight) {
            currentIndex = i;
        }
    }
    return currentIndex;
}

The - 1 in the bottom check is a small tolerance for sub-pixel rounding differences between browsers — without it, atBottom can sometimes fail to trigger by a fraction of a pixel.

Add this, then let's move on to real content — starting with Step 8: Host Cities (multi-column section), since that'll also help un-stick your page length and let you re-verify the indicator end-to-end with real content. Want to continue there next?

