            </div>
            <div class="city-card">
                <h3>New York/New Jersey</h3>
                <p>MetLife Stadium — hosts the final.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="city-card">
                <h3>Vancouver</h3>
                <p>BC Place — Canada's first World Cup matches.</p>
            </div>
            <!-- add more cities as you like -->
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

(Feel free to swap in real host cities/details you like better — there are 16 across the US/Mexico/Canada for 2026, pick however many you want to feature, minimum enough to clearly show 3+ columns, so 3-6 is reasonable.)

SCSS — the grid:

.city-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 24px;
}

.city-card {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
    border: 1px solid $border-color;
    padding: 24px;
    border-radius: 8px;
}

repeat(3, 1fr) creates exactly 3 equal-width columns. gap handles spacing between cards without needing margins (and without margin-collapse headaches like you just saw).

Something to think ahead about for Requirement #9 (responsiveness): 3 fixed columns will get cramped at 768×1024 (portrait tablet width). We'll handle that with a media query later once we do a responsiveness pass across the whole page — for now just get the 3-column grid working at normal desktop width, don't worry about small screens yet.

Add this, refresh, and confirm you see 3+ cards laid out in a row. Post back and then we'll do Step 9: the carousel next, which is the more involved JS piece.

