Step 9: Carousel (Highlights section)

This is the most JS-heavy piece after the navbar, so let's break it into layers again: markup → basic show/hide logic → arrow navigation → (later) polish like transitions.

Layer 1 — HTML structure

The standard carousel pattern: one wrapper, multiple "slide" children stacked in the same space, plus two arrow buttons.

<section id="highlights">
    <div class="container">
        <h2>Match Highlights</h2>
        <div class="carousel">
            <button class="carousel-arrow carousel-arrow-left" id="prev-slide">&#10094;</button>

            <div class="carousel-track">
                <div class="carousel-slide active">
                    <h3>Opening Match</h3>
                    <p>Mexico City kicks off the tournament at Estadio Azteca.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="carousel-slide">
                    <h3>Group Stage Upsets</h3>
                    <p>Underdogs make their mark across all three host nations.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="carousel-slide">
                    <h3>The Final</h3>
                    <p>MetLife Stadium hosts the culmination of the tournament.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <button class="carousel-arrow carousel-arrow-right" id="next-slide">&#10095;</button>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

Notice: only the first slide has the active class to start. That's the class your JS/CSS will use to control which one is visible.

Layer 2 — CSS: stack the slides, show only .active

The trick to a carousel: all slides occupy the same space (stacked via position: absolute inside a position: relative container), and only one is visible at a time via opacity/display.

.carousel {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 16px;
}

.carousel-track {
    position: relative;
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 200px; // reserve space so layout doesn't jump
}

.carousel-slide {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    width: 100%;
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
    transition: opacity 0.5s ease;

    &.active {
        opacity: 1;
        visibility: visible;
    }
}

.carousel-arrow {
    background: none;
    border: none;
    color: $text-color;
    font-size: 24px;
    cursor: pointer;
    padding: 8px;
}

That transition: opacity 0.5s ease is also a legitimate CSS3 animation you can point to for requirement #14, so worth keeping.

Try this much first — you should see the first slide's content, with two arrow buttons flanking it (arrows won't do anything yet, that's Layer 3). Confirm the layout looks right and post back before we wire up the JS click handlers — I want to make sure the absolute-positioning/stacking is behaving before adding the logic on top of it.

9/16/2026 13:48:38

Layer 3 — JS: arrow click handlers

The state you need to track: which slide index is currently active. Keep that in a single variable, and write one function that updates both the data and the DOM to match.

In main.js, add:

const slides = document.querySelectorAll('.carousel-slide');
const prevBtn = document.getElementById('prev-slide');
const nextBtn = document.getElementById('next-slide');
let currentSlide = 0;

function showSlide(index) {
    slides.forEach((slide, i) => {
        slide.classList.toggle('active', i === index);
    });
}

nextBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    currentSlide = (currentSlide + 1) % slides.length;
    showSlide(currentSlide);
});

prevBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    currentSlide = (currentSlide - 1 + slides.length) % slides.length;
    showSlide(currentSlide);
});

Walk through why the math works, since it's a common point of confusion:

classList.toggle('active', i === index) — the second argument to toggle is a boolean that forces add/remove instead of flipping blindly. So this line reads as "add active if this slide's index matches the target index, otherwise remove it" — applied to every slide in one pass, no manual if/else needed.

(currentSlide + 1) % slides.length — the % (modulo) wraps the index back to 0 once it would go past the last slide. E.g. with 3 slides: 2 + 1 = 3, 3 % 3 = 0 — wraps to the first slide instead of going out of bounds.

(currentSlide - 1 + slides.length) % slides.length — going backward from 0 would give -1, and JS's % doesn't wrap negative numbers the way you'd want (-1 % 3 is -1, not 2). Adding slides.length before the modulo shifts it back into positive range first, so it wraps correctly to the last slide.

Try this and click both arrows a few times — confirm it cycles forward and backward correctly, including wrapping past both ends (last→first going forward, first→last going backward). Post back once confirmed, and then we'll move to Step 10: the fixed-background-image section, which is a pure-CSS feature and a quick win after this.

9/16/2026 14:06:51

Good instinct — worth doing since your text color is light and photos vary in brightness.

The technique: CSS lets you stack multiple background images on one element, layered in the order you list them — the first one listed sits on top. So instead of just a photo, you layer a linear-gradient (acting as a semi-transparent black sheet) over the image, then the actual photo underneath.

Update the .bg-fixed rule:

.bg-fixed {
    background-image:
        linear-gradient(rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5), rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5)),
        url('../assets/stadium.jpg');
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
    background-attachment: fixed;
    background-color: $background-color;
    color: $text-color;
    min-height: 60vh;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
}

Walk through why this works:

linear-gradient(rgba(0,0,0,0.5), rgba(0,0,0,0.5)) — normally linear-gradient fades between two colors, but here both stops are the same color/opacity. So instead of a visible fade, you get a flat, uniform semi-transparent black sheet — a gradient with no actual gradient, used purely as a trick to get a "tintable" layer. The 0.5 is the opacity of the overlay (0 = invisible, 1 = solid black) — tune it to taste; 0.4–0.6 is a typical readable range.

Order matters: background-image takes a comma-separated list, and layers stack with the first one on top, last one on the bottom. So the gradient (first) sits above the photo (last) — exactly what you want, a tint on top of the image.

One background-size/background-position applies to both layers by default here since you didn't specify per-layer values — that's fine for this case since the gradient has no intrinsic size to worry about, it'll just stretch to cover along with the photo.

Refresh and confirm the text is now more readable against the image, and that the fixed-scroll effect still works correctly (this change doesn't affect that, just readability). Post back and then we'll move to Step 11: modals for the teams section.

9/16/2026 14:07:59

Step 11: Modals (Teams section)

Concept: a modal is just a hidden overlay element that you reveal via JS when triggered by a click, and hide again on a close action. No new CSS trick beyond display/visibility toggling plus fixed positioning to overlay the whole viewport.

Layer 1 — HTML

Add trigger buttons/cards for a few teams, plus one modal markup block per team (or one shared modal you repopulate with JS — let's start with the simpler "one modal per team" version first, since it needs no dynamic content-swapping logic):

<section id="teams">
    <div class="container">
        <h2>Teams</h2>
        <div class="team-grid">
            <div class="team-card" data-modal="modal-mexico">
                <h3>Mexico</h3>
                <p>View squad</p>
            </div>
            <div class="team-card" data-modal="modal-usa">
                <h3>USA</h3>
                <p>View squad</p>
            </div>
            <div class="team-card" data-modal="modal-canada">
                <h3>Canada</h3>
                <p>View squad</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<div class="modal" id="modal-mexico">
    <div class="modal-content">
        <span class="modal-close">&times;</span>
        <h3>Mexico</h3>
        <p>Host nation, three-time World Cup host (1970, 1986, 2026). Group stage details here.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="modal" id="modal-usa">
    <div class="modal-content">
        <span class="modal-close">&times;</span>
        <h3>United States</h3>
        <p>Co-host nation. Squad and group details here.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="modal" id="modal-canada">
    <div class="modal-content">
        <span class="modal-close">&times;</span>
        <h3>Canada</h3>
        <p>Co-host nation. Squad and group details here.</p>
    </div>
</div>

Notice the data-modal="modal-mexico" attribute on each card — that's a custom data attribute, a standard HTML5 way to attach extra info to an element for JS to read later (via element.dataset.modal), without hijacking a real attribute like id or class for that purpose.

Layer 2 — CSS: hidden by default, overlay when shown

.modal {
    display: none;
    position: fixed;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.7);
    z-index: 2000;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;

    &.open {
        display: flex;
    }
}

.modal-content {
    background-color: $nav-color;
    color: $text-color;
    padding: 32px;
    border-radius: 8px;
    max-width: 500px;
    position: relative;
}

.modal-close {
    position: absolute;
    top: 12px;
    right: 16px;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 24px;
}

Notice display: none by default, switching to display: flex via the .open class — using flex here (rather than block) is what makes align-items/justify-content center the .modal-content box both horizontally and vertically in the viewport. That's another good candidate to mention for your vertical-centering requirement.

Try adding just Layers 1–2 first — you won't be able to open the modal yet (no JS), but you can manually test it by temporarily adding class="modal open" to one modal in your HTML, refreshing, and confirming it overlays the page centered correctly. Remove that manual open class once confirmed, and post back — then we'll wire up Layer 3 (the click-to-open/close JS), which is short.

9/16/2026 14:13:28

Layer 3 — JS: open/close logic

The pattern: loop through all trigger cards, attach a click listener that reads the data-modal attribute to find the matching modal, and add the open class to it. Separately, wire up each modal's close button (and clicking the dark overlay itself) to remove that class.

Add to main.js:

const teamCards = document.querySelectorAll('.team-card');
const modals = document.querySelectorAll('.modal');

teamCards.forEach((card) => {
    card.addEventListener('click', () => {
        const modalId = card.dataset.modal;
        document.getElementById(modalId).classList.add('open');
    });
});

modals.forEach((modal) => {
    const closeBtn = modal.querySelector('.modal-close');

    closeBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        modal.classList.remove('open');
    });

    // Close if user clicks the dark overlay outside the content box
    modal.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
        if (event.target === modal) {
            modal.classList.remove('open');
        }
    });
});

Walk through the trickier bit — the overlay-click check:

event.target === modal — when you click anywhere inside the modal (including on .modal-content or its children), event.target is whatever specific element was actually clicked, which bubbles up through the DOM to your listener on .modal. So this check asks: "was the actual click target the outer .modal div itself, and not something nested inside it?" That's true only when you click the dark backdrop area, not the white content box — which is the expected UX (clicking outside closes it, clicking the content doesn't).

card.dataset.modal reads the data-modal="modal-mexico" attribute you set in the HTML — camelCase dataset.modal maps to the hyphenated data-modal attribute automatically, that's the standard convention for all data-* attributes.

Also worth adding (small UX touch, not required but easy): closing on the Escape key.

document.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
    if (event.key === 'Escape') {
        modals.forEach((modal) => modal.classList.remove('open'));
    }
});

Try clicking a team card, confirm the modal opens centered over a dark overlay, then confirm both the × button and clicking outside the content box close it. Post back once verified — then Step 12: the HTML5 video section, which is short, followed by the About section content and a full responsiveness pass across every section (the remaining big piece).

9/16/2026 14:34:31

Step 13: About section content

Quick one — this section just needs real intro text and it's a good spot to demonstrate vertical centering again if you want a second example, though one is technically enough for the requirement.

HTML:

<section id="about">
    <div class="container">
        <h2>About the Tournament</h2>
        <p>
            The 2026 FIFA World Cup marks the first tournament hosted jointly by three nations —
            the United States, Mexico, and Canada — and the first to feature an expanded 48-team format.
            Matches will be played across 16 host cities from June to July 2026.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

Feel free to expand with more real details (format, dates, number of matches, ticket info) — this is just a starting skeleton. No new CSS needed here beyond what .container already gives you, unless you want to style the heading differently, which is up to you.

Step 14: Responsiveness pass

This is the biggest remaining structural step. Rather than write a media query per section, let's go through what's actually likely to break at each required resolution and fix them in one pass.

The five target resolutions, grouped by what matters:

1920×1080, 1366×768, 1280×720 — all comfortably wider than your $container-width: 1200px, so these should already look fine with no changes, since .container's max-width handles them.

1024×768 — narrower than 1200px, so .container will correctly shrink to 100% width (good, you already built that in Step 2) — but check whether anything inside the container (like your 3-column city grid) gets cramped.

768×1024 — this is a portrait/tall orientation, notably narrower than 1024px. This is where things are most likely to visibly break: your 3-column grid, your nav links (6 of them in a row might wrap awkwardly or overflow), and your carousel arrows/spacing.

The tool: media queries, keyed off max-width. A common approach — define one breakpoint for "narrow" screens (below ~900px, catching your 768px case) and adjust the specific things that break:

@media (max-width: 900px) {
    .city-grid {
        grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); // drop to 2 columns
    }

    nav ul {
        gap: 12px; // tighter spacing so 6 links fit
        font-size: 14px;
    }
}

@media (max-width: 600px) {
    .city-grid {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr; // single column on very narrow screens
    }
}

Your task: open your dev tools' responsive design mode (Firefox: the same Ctrl+Shift+K panel has a "Responsive Design Mode" toggle, or Ctrl+Shift+M), and manually punch in each of the 5 required resolutions one at a time. For each one, check:

Does anything overflow horizontally (a scrollbar appearing on the x-axis is usually a sign something's too wide)?

Does the nav wrap or overlap awkwardly?

Does the city grid or carousel look cramped?

Report back what specifically breaks at which resolution (if anything) — rather than me guessing every possible issue upfront, let's fix the actual problems you observe, since your exact content/spacing choices will determine what needs adjusting.

9/16/2026 14:46:59

Good — a horizontal scrollbar/overflow at narrow widths is one of the most common responsive bugs. Let's narrow down which element is causing it, since "too wide" could be coming from several places.

First, identify the culprit before guessing at fixes. Open dev tools while in responsive mode at 768×1024, and try this in the console:

document.querySelectorAll('*').forEach(el => {
    if (el.scrollWidth > document.documentElement.clientWidth) {
        console.log(el);
    }
});

This logs every element on the page whose actual content width exceeds the viewport's width — i.e. the specific element(s) pushing the page wider than the screen. Whatever gets logged is your culprit(s).

A few usual suspects worth checking specifically, based on what we've built:

The video element — you set max-width: 800px, but if its intrinsic video file resolution is wider than the viewport and something's off with the width: 100% inheritance (e.g. if it's not actually inside .container), it can overflow.

The nav <ul> — 6 links with gap: 24px (or even the tightened 12px from the media query) might just not fit in 768px width and could be wrapping or pushing wider than expected, especially combined with the container's own horizontal padding.

Long unbreakable text or a fixed-width element — anything with an explicit width in pixels rather than %/max-width that's wider than 768px.

The .carousel — the flex row with two arrow buttons plus the track; if the track's min-height/content forces extra width somewhere.

Run that console snippet and tell me what element(s) it logs — that'll tell us exactly where to focus the fix rather than patching things one at a time by guesswork.

9/16/2026 14:54:49

That's a different — and telling — symptom. Overflow at your widest resolution (wider than your own $container-width: 1200px) means it's probably not a "content too big for a narrow screen" issue at all. It's more likely something with an explicit width that's slightly larger than the viewport regardless of screen size, or a stray margin/padding pushing an element past the edge.

Run the same diagnostic snippet at 1920×1080 and see what it flags:

document.querySelectorAll('*').forEach(el => {
    if (el.scrollWidth > document.documentElement.clientWidth) {
        console.log(el);
    }
});

A specific suspect worth checking first, given what you just changed: your nav ul media query sets gap: 12px at max-width: 900px — that shouldn't apply at 1920px, so ignore that. But think about your base (non-media-query) nav styles — is there anything computing to more than 100vw? A classic cause: width: 100% on a position: fixed element combined with any horizontal padding/border on that same element — width: 100% refers to 100% of the containing block (the viewport, for fixed), and if you then add padding on top of that 100% width without box-sizing: border-box applying correctly, the total rendered width exceeds the viewport by the padding amount. You do have the global box-sizing: border-box reset, so nav itself should be fine — but double check nothing is overriding that unexpectedly.

Another common cause at exactly this kind of "wide screens only" scenario: a native OS/browser scrollbar taking up width. 100vw (if you used it anywhere) includes the scrollbar width in some browsers, causing a width: 100vw element to be very slightly wider than the visible area once a vertical scrollbar appears — creating a small horizontal overflow as a side effect. Search your SCSS for any use of 100vw — do you have one? (I don't recall giving you that in any of the snippets so far, so if it's there, you likely added it yourself somewhere.)

Run the console check and let me know what it logs — that'll tell us definitively rather than guessing further.

