That's odd — if overflow-x: hidden on body didn't hide the scrollbar, that tells us something useful: the overflow might be happening at the <html> level, not inside body. Some fixed-position elements (like your nav and .modal) are positioned relative to the viewport itself, which can cause overflow that body's own overflow-x doesn't contain — you'd need it on html instead.

Try this instead:

html {
    overflow-x: hidden;
}

(Keep scroll-behavior: smooth on the same html rule, just add the property.)

Separately, let's get harder numbers instead of relying on that querySelectorAll scan — run these three lines individually in the console and tell me all three results:

console.log(document.documentElement.scrollWidth, document.documentElement.clientWidth);
console.log(document.body.scrollWidth, document.body.clientWidth);
console.log(document.querySelector('nav').getBoundingClientRect().width, document.documentElement.clientWidth);

Line 1 tells us if the <html> element itself thinks its content is wider than the visible viewport (this is the actual authoritative check for "is there horizontal overflow at all").

Line 2 does the same for <body>.

Line 3 checks specifically whether your fixed nav (a prime suspect since it's width: 100% + position: fixed) is rendering wider than the viewport.

If line 1 shows scrollWidth > clientWidth, we've confirmed real overflow exists and roughly by how many pixels (the difference between the two numbers) — that number can also be a clue (e.g. if it's ~15-17px, that's almost certainly the scrollbar-width theory).

Paste back all three results.

9/16/2026 15:22:40

Fixing a layout issue with off-center page content.

Fixing a layout issue with off-center page content.

Done

Ah — that's not a centering bug, it's a missing-padding one. Here's why: your .container has width: 100% and max-width: 1200px, but no horizontal padding. At the two smallest resolutions (1024×768 and 768×1024), the viewport is narrower than 1200px, so .container shrinks to fill the entire viewport width — and since it has zero left/right padding, its content butts directly against the screen edges. It's technically still "centered" (there's nothing to center within, since the container itself is the full width), it's just missing breathing room.

Fix: add horizontal padding to .container so content never touches the raw edge, even when the container is full-width:

.container {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: $container-width;
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
    padding-left: 24px;
    padding-right: 24px;
    color: $text-color;
}

This is a very standard pattern — .container almost always carries some horizontal padding precisely for this reason (it's often called a "gutter"). At wide screens it's barely noticeable since the max-width cap dominates; at narrow screens it's what keeps text from looking like it's falling off the page.

Add that, re-check the two narrow resolutions in Chrome's device mode, and confirm there's now visible space on both sides of your content. Post back and then we'll move to the last remaining pieces: footer content with social icons, and a final pass checking off all 16 requirements against what you've actually built.

9/16/2026 15:25:07

Great — that closes out the responsiveness pass. Let's finish the last two items.

Step 15: Footer — social icons

You've already got the FontAwesome CDN link loaded from Step 1, so this is mostly markup + a little styling. FontAwesome icons work by adding specific classes to an empty <i> tag — the class itself renders the icon via CSS (a good, simple example of "scalable vector icons through CSS" for requirement #15, satisfying #16 at the same time).

HTML — inside footer .container:

<footer>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="social-icons">
            <a href="https://twitter.com" aria-label="Twitter"><i class="fa-brands fa-twitter"></i></a>
            <a href="https://instagram.com" aria-label="Instagram"><i class="fa-brands fa-instagram"></i></a>
            <a href="https://youtube.com" aria-label="YouTube"><i class="fa-brands fa-youtube"></i></a>
        </div>
        <p>&copy; 2026 FIFA World Cup Fan Site. Built for educational purposes.</p>
    </div>
</footer>

A couple of notes:

aria-label on each link gives screen readers a text description, since the link itself contains no readable text (just an icon) — good practice whenever you communicate meaning through an icon alone.

fa-brands is the FontAwesome prefix for brand/company logos (as opposed to fa-solid for generic icons) — check FontAwesome's site if a specific icon name doesn't render, class names occasionally differ from what you'd guess.

SCSS:

.social-icons {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 24px;
    margin-bottom: 16px;

    a {
        color: $text-color;
        font-size: 24px;
        transition: color 0.3s ease;

        &:hover {
            color: $primary-accent;
        }
    }
}

footer {
    text-align: center;
}

That :hover transition is another small CSS3-animation touchpoint, and justify-content: center handles the horizontal-centering requirement for this stripe consistent with the rest of your page.

Add this, confirm the icons render (not empty boxes — empty/missing boxes usually mean the FontAwesome CDN link didn't load or the class name is wrong), and post back. Then we'll do the final requirements checklist — going through all 16 items one by one against what you've built, to catch anything missed before you consider this done.

9/16/2026 16:01:35

Good change — that's a common navbar pattern (brand on left, links on right) and just needs a flex layout tweak plus one new element.

HTML — add a brand/logo element alongside your <ul>, both inside the same .container:

<nav>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="nav-brand">FIFA World Cup 2026</div>
        <ul>
            <li><a href="#about">About</a></li>
            <li><a href="#host-cities">Host Cities</a></li>
            <li><a href="#highlights">Highlights</a></li>
            <li><a href="#stadiums">Stadiums</a></li>
            <li><a href="#teams">Teams</a></li>
            <li><a href="#video">Video</a></li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</nav>

SCSS — right now .container has no display set, so .nav-brand and ul are just stacking as normal block elements (one under the other). Make .container a flex row specifically within nav, and push the two children to opposite ends:

nav .container {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
}

Walk through why: justify-content: space-between pushes the first and last flex children to the opposite edges of the container, with any extra space distributed between them — exactly "brand on the left, links on the right" with just two children. align-items: center vertically aligns the brand text and the link list so they sit on the same baseline, since they may have different heights.

One thing to watch: you're scoping this flex rule to nav .container specifically, not the base .container class — because your other sections' .container (About, Host Cities, etc.) shouldn't become flex rows, they should stay block-centered as before. Scoping via the descendant selector (nav .container) keeps this change isolated to just the navbar without affecting every other section.

You may also want a small style on .nav-brand itself (font-weight, size) so it doesn't look like plain body text:

.nav-brand {
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 1.1em;
    color: $text-color;
}

Try this, refresh, and confirm the brand sits left and links sit right, vertically aligned. Post back and then let's do that final requirements checklist.

