$container-width: 1200px;
$section-padding: 80px;

$background-color: #2A398D;
$primary-accent: #E61D25;
$text-color: #D1D4D1;
$border-color: #3CAC3B;
$nav-color: #474A4A;

$font-family: Huninn;

html{
    scroll-behavior: smooth;
    overflow-x: hidden;
}

*{
    box-sizing: border-box;
}

body{
    font-family: $font-family;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    background-color: $background-color;
}

nav{
    position: fixed;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    width: 100%;
    background-color: $nav-color;
    z-index: 1000;
    padding: 20px 0;
    transition: padding 0.3s ease, font-size 0.3s ease;
    font-size: 20px;

    &.scrolled{
        padding: 8px 0;
        font-size: 14px;
    }
}

nav a{
    color: $text-color;
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 600;

    &.active{
        color: $primary-accent;
    }
}

nav .container{
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
}

.nav-brand{
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 1.1em;
    color: $text-color;
}

ul{
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    gap: 24px;
    display: flex;
}

.container{
    width: 100%;
    max-width: $container-width;
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
    padding-left: 24px;
    padding-right: 24px;
    color: $text-color;
}

header{
    padding-top: 100px;
    padding-bottom: $section-padding;
}

section{
    padding-top: $section-padding;
    padding-bottom: $section-padding;
}

.city-grid{
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 24px;
}

.city-card{
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
    border: 3px solid $border-color;
    padding: 24px;
    border-radius: 8px;
}

.carousel{
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 16px;
}

.carousel-track{
    position: relative;
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 200px; // reserve space so layout doesn't jump
}

.carousel-slide{
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    width: 100%;
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
    transition: opacity 0.5s ease;

    &.active{
        opacity: 1;
        visibility: visible;
    }
}

.carousel-arrow{
    background: none;
    border: none;
    color: $text-color;
    font-size: 24px;
    cursor: pointer;
    padding: 8px;
}

.bg-fixed{
    background-image:
        linear-gradient(rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5), rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5)),
        url('../assets/wcstadiums.webp');
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
    background-attachment: fixed;
    background-color: $background-color;
    color: $text-color;
    min-height: 60vh;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
}

.modal{
    display: none;
    position: fixed;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.7);
    z-index: 2000;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;

    &.open {
        display: flex;
    }
}

.modal-content{
    background-color: $nav-color;
    color: $text-color;
    padding: 32px;
    border-radius: 8px;
    max-width: 500px;
    position: relative;
}

.modal-close{
    position: absolute;
    top: 12px;
    right: 16px;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 24px;
}

video{
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 800px;
    display: block;
    margin: 0 auto; // horizontally centers it within .container
    border-radius: 8px;
}

.social-icons {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 24px;
    margin-bottom: 16px;

    a {
        color: $text-color;
        font-size: 24px;
        transition: color 0.3s ease;

        &:hover {
            color: $primary-accent;
        }
    }
}

footer{
    padding-top: $section-padding;
    padding-bottom: $section-padding;
    text-align: center;
}

@media(max-width: 900px){
    .city-grid{
        grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); // drop to 2 columns
    }

    nav ul{
        gap: 12px; // tighter spacing so 6 links fit
        font-size: 14px;
    }
}

@media(max-width: 600px){
    .city-grid{
        grid-template-columns: 1fr; // single column on very narrow screens
    }
}

lets unify font sizes across the whole site

column2:
9/16/2026 16:08:39

Good goal — right now you've got ad-hoc sizes scattered around (font-size: 20px on nav, 1.1em on nav-brand, 24px on icons/arrows, unstyled default browser sizes for h2/h3/p elsewhere). Let's set up a proper type scale with SCSS variables so every size traces back to one place.

Step 1 — Define a font-size scale as variables, near your other variables at the top:

$font-size-sm: 0.875rem;  // 14px equivalent — small text, nav links when scrolled
$font-size-base: 1rem;    // 16px equivalent — body text, paragraphs
$font-size-lg: 1.25rem;   // 20px equivalent — nav brand, larger UI text
$font-size-h3: 1.5rem;    // 24px equivalent — card/modal subheadings
$font-size-h2: 2rem;      // 32px equivalent — section headings
$font-size-h1: 3rem;      // 48px equivalent — hero title

Why rem instead of px: rem units are relative to the root (html) element's font-size, rather than a fixed pixel value. This matters for accessibility — if a user has changed their browser's default font size (common for visually-impaired users), rem-based sizes scale with that preference automatically, while px values ignore it entirely. It's a small thing, but it's the modern default practice and easy to justify in a write-up. (1rem = 16px by default in virtually all browsers unless you've explicitly changed the root font-size, which you haven't — so the px-equivalent comments above are accurate as a mental model.)

Step 2 — Replace every hardcoded size with the matching variable. Go through your file and swap:

nav { font-size: 20px; } → font-size: $font-size-lg;

nav.scrolled { font-size: 14px; } → font-size: $font-size-sm;

.nav-brand { font-size: 1.1em; } → font-size: $font-size-lg; (drop the em, now consistent with everything else)

.carousel-arrow { font-size: 24px; } → font-size: $font-size-h3;

.social-icons a { font-size: 24px; } → font-size: $font-size-h3;

body { } → add font-size: $font-size-base; explicitly (right now it's relying on the browser default, which happens to match, but stating it explicitly makes the scale self-documenting)

Step 3 — Your h2/h3 elements have no rules at all right now, meaning they're using the browser's built-in default sizes (which vary slightly by browser). Add explicit rules so every heading across every section is consistent:

h2 {
    font-size: $font-size-h2;
    margin-bottom: 24px; // consistent spacing below section titles too, while you're at it
}

h3 {
    font-size: $font-size-h3;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
}

This one change alone will make About, Host Cities, Highlights, Stadiums, Teams, and Video all share identical heading sizes — right now each is silently relying on browser defaults, so they happen to look consistent, but it's not something you're actually controlling.

A design note before you commit to exact numbers: your hero (<header>) currently just contains plain text with no explicit sizing — do you want "FIFA World Cup 2026" in the header to be your biggest text on the page ($font-size-h1)? If so, you'll want to wrap it in an actual <h1> tag rather than bare text in the <div class="container">, both semantically (there should be exactly one h1 per page, and this is the natural spot for it) and so this scale applies to it correctly.

Make these swaps, wrap your hero text in <h1>, and post back both files — then we'll do the final requirements checklist.

9/16/2026 16:47:40

Good addition — but heads up: the assignment explicitly bans inline styling, so we can't just do style="background-image: url(...)" directly on each card even though that'd be the quickest way. We need a way to give each card a different image while keeping all styling in your SCSS file.

The clean way: one class per city, each with its own background rule.

HTML — add a second class per card, one unique to that city:

<div class="city-card city-card--mexico">
    <h3>Mexico City</h3>
    <p>Estadio Azteca - 87,523</p>
</div>

<div class="city-card city-card--nyc">
    <h3>New York/New Jersey</h3>
    <p>MetLife Stadium - hosts the final.</p>
</div>

<div class="city-card city-card--vancouver">
    <h3>Vancouver</h3>
    <p>BC Place - Canada's first World Cup matches.</p>
</div>

(The city-card--mexico naming style — base class plus a --modifier — is a common convention called BEM, useful for exactly this situation: same base styling, one specific variant per instance.)

SCSS — keep the shared card styling in .city-card as-is, and add the per-city image only in each modifier class:

.city-card {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
    border: 3px solid $border-color;
    padding: 24px;
    border-radius: 8px;
    color: $text-color; // make sure text stays readable over any photo
    min-height: 200px; // give the background image room to actually show

    &--mexico {
        background-image: linear-gradient(rgba(0,0,0,0.5), rgba(0,0,0,0.5)), url('../assets/mexico-city.jpg');
    }

    &--nyc {
        background-image: linear-gradient(rgba(0,0,0,0.5), rgba(0,0,0,0.5)), url('../assets/nyc.jpg');
    }

    &--vancouver {
        background-image: linear-gradient(rgba(0,0,0,0.5), rgba(0,0,0,0.5)), url('../assets/vancouver.jpg');
    }
}

Note the &--mexico syntax — SCSS nesting compiles this to .city-card--mexico automatically (the & refers back to the parent selector), and reuses the same dark-overlay trick from your Stadiums section for text readability, since you're putting light text over a photo again.

Drop three actual image files into assets/ matching those paths, refresh, and confirm each card shows a different photo with readable text on top. Post back once you've got all three (or however many cities you're featuring) — then let's finally run that requirements checklist.

9/16/2026 17:14:11

Good addition — makes the carousel much more visual. Let's think about layout first: do you want the image above the text (stacked) or beside it (side-by-side)? I'll show stacked since it's simpler and works better at narrow widths, but the pattern is easy to adapt either way.

HTML — add an <img> inside each slide:

<div class="carousel-track">
    <div class="carousel-slide active">
        <img src="assets/highlights/opening-match.jpg" alt="Opening match at Estadio Azteca">
        <h3>Opening Match</h3>
        <p>Mexico City kicks off the tournament at Estadio Azteca.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="carousel-slide">
        <img src="assets/highlights/group-stage.jpg" alt="Group stage action">
        <h3>Group Stage Upsets</h3>
        <p>Underdogs make their mark across all three host nations.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="carousel-slide">
        <img src="assets/highlights/final.jpg" alt="World Cup final at MetLife Stadium">
        <h3>The Final</h3>
        <p>MetLife Stadium hosts the culmination of the tournament.</p>
    </div>
</div>

SCSS — scope the image rule to .carousel-slide img specifically (not the bare img tag globally, since you already have per-purpose image classes elsewhere like .about-image and don't want them fighting):

.carousel-slide {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    width: 100%;
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
    transition: opacity 0.5s ease;

    img {
        width: 100%;
        max-height: 280px;
        object-fit: cover;
        border-radius: 8px;
        margin-bottom: 16px;
    }

    &.active {
        opacity: 1;
        visibility: visible;
    }
}

The one new property here worth understanding: object-fit: cover. Since your three images likely have different aspect ratios/dimensions, just setting width: 100% alone would make them different heights, causing the carousel to visually jump as you flip between slides. object-fit: cover forces the image to fill the fixed max-height box you gave it while cropping (not stretching/distorting) to maintain its own aspect ratio — so all three slides end up with a uniform image size regardless of the source file's actual dimensions.

One thing to double check afterward: your .carousel-track currently has min-height: 200px — with images now added, slides are taller than that. Bump it up (e.g. min-height: 400px) so the track reserves enough space for the tallest slide and nothing gets clipped or causes a layout jump when switching slides.

Drop three images into assets/highlights/, adjust the min-height, refresh, and click through the arrows to confirm each slide shows its image without the carousel height jumping around. Post back and then let's finally run that full requirements checklist.

9/16/2026 17:41:20

Good UX instinct — right now nothing signals interactivity beyond the "View squad" text. Let's add a few standard visual cues that signal "clickable" without needing any new JS.

1. cursor: pointer — the single most important signal. Right now team cards probably show the default arrow cursor on hover, which subconsciously reads as "not interactive."

2. A hover state — some visible change when the mouse is over the card, confirming "yes, this responds to you."

3. A subtle lift/scale animation on hover — reinforces the same idea with motion, and doubles as another CSS3 animation example for your requirements checklist.

SCSS — find your (currently probably minimal) .team-card rule and expand it:

.team-card {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
    border: 2px solid $border-color;
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 24px;
    text-align: center;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: transform 0.3s ease, background-color 0.3s ease, border-color 0.3s ease;

    &:hover {
        transform: translateY(-6px);
        background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
        border-color: $primary-accent;
    }
}

Walk through what's happening:

cursor: pointer overrides the default arrow cursor with the "hand" cursor on hover — the universal browser signal for "this is clickable."

transform: translateY(-6px) on :hover shifts the card up slightly, combined with the transition on transform so it animates smoothly rather than snapping.

Changing background-color/border-color together gives a clearer "lit up" feel than motion alone.

Also worth doing: make the "View squad" text itself look more like a call-to-action, rather than plain body text:

.team-card p {
    color: $primary-accent;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-top: 8px;
}

That reuses your accent color, so it visually matches your .active nav link and other interactive-accent elements already on the page — consistent signal across the site that "accent color = this does something."

Add both rules, refresh, and hover over a team card to confirm you see the lift + color change + pointer cursor together. Post back — and unless there's anything else you want to add, this is a good point to actually run through the full 16-requirement checklist against everything you've built, to catch anything missed before you call it done.

9/16/2026 17:45:14

Good catch — makes sense why: your nav.scrolled rule sets font-size: $font-size-sm on the nav element itself, and CSS font-size is inherited by children by default... but .nav-brand has its own explicit font-size: $font-size-lg rule, which overrides whatever it would've inherited from its parent. Explicit rules always win over inheritance, regardless of nesting order.

Fix: make .nav-brand's size respond to the same .scrolled state, same pattern as your other scroll-driven size changes:

.nav-brand {
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: $font-size-lg;
    transition: font-size 0.3s ease;
}

nav.scrolled .nav-brand {
    font-size: $font-size-base;
}

Walk through why this works: nav.scrolled .nav-brand is a descendant selector — it targets .nav-brand elements that live inside an element matching nav.scrolled. Since your JS already toggles the scrolled class on nav itself (from Step 6), this rule automatically activates the moment that class is present — no new JS needed, you're just adding a new CSS consequence to a state you're already tracking.

I picked $font-size-base as the "scrolled" size for the brand (a modest shrink) rather than matching it exactly to $font-size-sm (which is what your nav links shrink to) — brand text is often kept slightly larger than nav links even when small, since it's your primary identity element. Feel free to use $font-size-sm instead if you want it to shrink by the same proportion as the links; either is a reasonable design choice, not a correctness issue.

Add the transition too, so it animates smoothly in sync with your existing nav padding/font transition rather than snapping abruptly. Refresh, scroll, and confirm both the brand and the nav links now shrink together. Post back — and once this is confirmed, let's actually do that full requirements checklist, since the build itself sounds essentially complete at this point.

9/16/2026 17:52:09

Rethinking scope toward a scalable, data-driven modal design.

Rethinking scope toward a scalable, data-driven modal design.

Done

Before diving in — 48 static modal blocks (like you have now) would mean ~48 nearly-identical <div class="modal"> blocks in your HTML, each with 26 names. That's a huge amount of duplicated markup, hard to maintain, and honestly more content than the assignment needs (it only asks for "a section which uses modal windows with additional content" — a handful of teams already satisfies that requirement).

Given you want to go big anyway, this is actually the right moment to switch to a data-driven approach: store all the squad data in one JS object, and have a single modal in your HTML that gets repopulated dynamically depending on which team card was clicked. This scales to 48 teams (or 8) with zero extra HTML — you're only ever adding data, not markup. It's also a stronger thing to point to for your JS requirement than 48 copy-pasted static blocks.

Step 1 — One modal in HTML instead of one per team:

<div class="modal" id="team-modal">
    <div class="modal-content">
        <span class="modal-close">&times;</span>
        <h3 id="modal-team-name"></h3>
        <div class="squad-list" id="modal-squad-list"></div>
    </div>
</div>

Step 2 — Team cards reference a key, same as before:

<div class="team-card" data-team="mexico"><h3>Mexico</h3><p>View squad</p></div>
<div class="team-card" data-team="usa"><h3>United States</h3><p>View squad</p></div>
<div class="team-card" data-team="canada"><h3>Canada</h3><p>View squad</p></div>
<!-- one card per team you want to feature -->

Step 3 — A JS data object holding every squad, keyed by the same string:

const teams = {
    mexico: {
        name: 'Mexico',
        GK: ['Raúl Rangel', 'Carlos Acevedo', 'Guillermo Ochoa'],
        DF: ['Jorge Sánchez', 'César Montes', 'Edson Álvarez (captain)', 'Johan Vásquez', 'Israel Reyes', 'Mateo Chávez', 'Jesús Gallardo'],
        MF: ['Érik Lira', 'Luis Romo', 'Álvaro Fidalgo', 'Orbelín Pineda', 'Obed Vargas', 'Gilberto Mora', 'Luis Chávez', 'Brian Gutiérrez'],
        FW: ['Raúl Jiménez', 'Alexis Vega', 'Santiago Giménez', 'Armando González', 'Julián Quiñones', 'César Huerta', 'Guillermo Martínez', 'Roberto Alvarado']
    },
    usa: {
        name: 'United States',
        GK: ['Matt Turner', 'Matt Freese', 'Chris Brady'],
