Good catch — that's leftover from the old multi-modal setup. Now that you're down to a single teamModal, the fix is simpler than the original:

document.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
    if (event.key === 'Escape') {
        teamModal.classList.remove('open');
    }
});

The old version looped over a modals NodeList (from when you had one <div class="modal"> per team) and removed .open from every one of them — but you no longer have that NodeList at all since Step "one modal for all teams." Now there's just one modal element to worry about, so no loop is needed — directly target teamModal and remove the class.

One thing worth double-checking while you're in this area: make sure you also removed the old per-team modal open/close code (the modals.forEach(...) block with closeBtn/overlay-click listeners from before) — that referenced a modals NodeList that queried multiple .modal elements, which no longer matches your new single-modal HTML structure. If that old code is still sitting in your main.js alongside the new teamModal-based listeners, it'll either throw an error (if modals is undefined) or silently do nothing (if document.querySelectorAll('.modal') now just returns a list with one item, which technically still works but is redundant with your new dedicated listeners).

Can you paste your current full main.js? I want to make sure there isn't a mix of old and new modal code causing conflicts, rather than just patching this one function in isolation.

