Reviewing SCSS features and converting remaining plain CSS.

Reviewing SCSS features and converting remaining plain CSS.

Done

Good question — let's inventory what you're already using, then look at spots that are still "CSS in a .scss file" that could showcase more SCSS-specific features.

What you're already using

Variables ($container-width, $font-size-*, $primary-accent, etc.) — the whole file's built on these.

Nesting — nav { &.scrolled {...} }, .team-card { &:hover {...} }, .carousel-slide { img {...}, &.active {...} }, .squad-list { h4 {...}, p {...} }, nav.scrolled .nav-brand.

A SCSS map — $city-images: (...).

@each loop over a map — generating all 16 .city-card--* background rules.

String interpolation — #{$name}, #{$file} inside the @each.

That's already five distinct features, more than enough to satisfy "utilize SCSS features" — but there's room to demonstrate a couple more (mixins, placeholders, nested media queries), which also happen to remove some real repetition in your file. Here's where:

1. A mixin for your repeated "dark overlay on background image" pattern

You write this exact gradient in two places (.city-card's @each loop and .bg-fixed):

linear-gradient(rgba(0,0,0,0.5), rgba(0,0,0,0.5))

Turn it into a mixin that takes the opacity as a parameter:

@mixin dark-overlay($opacity: 0.5) {
    background-image: linear-gradient(rgba(0, 0, 0, $opacity), rgba(0, 0, 0, $opacity)), var(--bg-image);
}

Actually, since the image itself differs per use, a cleaner version just returns the gradient layer and lets you compose it:

@function overlay($opacity: 0.5) {
    @return linear-gradient(rgba(0, 0, 0, $opacity), rgba(0, 0, 0, $opacity));
}

Then:

.bg-fixed {
    background-image: overlay(), url('../assets/wcstadiums.webp');
    // ...
}

.city-card {
    // ...
    @each $name, $file in $city-images {
        &--#{$name} {
            background-image: overlay(), url('../assets/#{$file}');
        }
    }
}

This is a SCSS function (@function/@return) — a new feature beyond what you have, and it removes the duplicated gradient string.

2. A mixin for your repeated transition declarations

You write multi-property transition lists by hand in nav, .nav-brand, .carousel-slide, .team-card, .social-icons a. A small mixin cuts the repetition and reads more clearly at each call site:

@mixin transition($properties...) {
    transition: $properties;
}

Usage stays almost identical to what you have now:

.team-card {
    // ...
    @include transition(transform 0.3s ease, background-color 0.3s ease, border-color 0.3s ease);
}

This one's a modest win — worth doing mainly to demonstrate @mixin/@include explicitly, since you don't have one anywhere yet, and that's a very standard rubric item to point at.

3. A placeholder + @extend for your repeated section-padding pattern

header, section, and footer all independently declare the same padding-top/padding-bottom pair. A placeholder selector is SCSS's tool for "shared styles with no output of their own":

%stripe-padding {
    padding-top: $section-padding;
    padding-bottom: $section-padding;
}

header {
    @extend %stripe-padding;
    padding-top: 100px; // override for nav clearance
}

section {
    @extend %stripe-padding;
}

footer {
    @extend %stripe-padding;
}

@extend is different from a mixin: instead of copying the properties into each rule (like @include would), it merges the selectors together in the compiled CSS (header, section, footer { padding-top: ...; padding-bottom: ...; }) — smaller output, and a good thing to be able to explain if asked "what's the difference between @extend and @include" in a write-up.

4. Nest your media queries inside the selector instead of at the bottom of the file

Right now your two @media blocks sit at the very end, disconnected from the rules they override — you have to jump around the file to see all the responsive behavior for .city-grid. SCSS lets you nest a media query inside the selector it modifies:

.city-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    gap: 24px;

    @media (max-width: 900px) {
        grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }

    @media (max-width: 600px) {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
}

Same compiled output, but now everything about .city-grid's responsiveness lives in one place. You could do the same for nav ul's media-query rule, nesting it inside your existing ul { ... } block.

None of this changes any visual behavior — it's a refactor, not a feature addition to the page. But it demonstrates @function, @mixin/@include, @extend/placeholders, and nested @media, on top of what you already have (variables, nesting, maps, @each, interpolation) — a genuinely broad spread of SCSS features to cite if your write-up asks you to list which ones you used and why.

Want me to fold all four of these into your full main.scss as one consolidated file, or apply them one at a time so you can verify nothing breaks after each change?

