Diagnosing off-center layout at a specific tablet resolution.

Diagnosing off-center layout at a specific tablet resolution.

Done

Good catch — and this one's likely isolated to a specific culprit rather than a general centering bug, since your .container centering logic (margin: auto + padding) is resolution-independent and already verified working elsewhere.

Think about what's unique to your page at exactly this width. You have two grids using repeat(4, 1fr) — .city-grid and .team-grid — but only .city-grid has media queries dropping it to 2 then 1 column at narrower widths. .team-grid has no responsive rule at all — it's locked to 4 columns at every screen size, including 768px.

Why that causes exactly the symptom you're describing: 48 cards in 4 rigid columns, each with padding: 24px and gap: 24px, need real minimum width per column. At 768px total viewport width (minus your container's padding-left/padding-right: 24px each side), there may not be enough room for 4 columns without either squeezing cards uncomfortably small or overflowing. Since you have overflow-x: hidden on html, any overflow doesn't show as a scrollbar — it just gets silently clipped. The visible result: content that's actually wider than the viewport, with everything shifted left because you're only seeing the left portion of a wider row that would otherwise center.

Confirm this before fixing it — same diagnostic approach as before. At 768×1024, run in the console:

document.querySelector('.team-grid').scrollWidth

Compare that to document.documentElement.clientWidth. If the grid's width is meaningfully larger than the viewport, that confirms it's the cause.

The fix, assuming that's confirmed — add the same responsive pattern you already built for .city-grid, nested the same way:

.team-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    gap: 24px;

    @media (max-width: 900px) {
        grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }

    @media (max-width: 600px) {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
}

Run the console check first to confirm the diagnosis, then add this and re-test at 768×1024 — the content should center correctly once the grid stops trying to force 4 columns into a space that can't comfortably hold them.
